	<article class="content container">
		<p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
		<h1>Playing in Time</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Anyone can learn to make the right chord shape. The skill that separates "playing the
			notes" from "playing the song" is timing — hitting each target shape exactly when the
			chart says so. This guide is a three-stage drill for getting there.
		</p>

		<h2>Why timing is the real skill</h2>
		<p>
			Gesture Synth is unusual: your hands are not the instrument, they are the controller.
			The chord sounds the moment the engine detects your shape, so there is no physical
			delay to hide behind — you either arrive at the chord on the beat or you do not. The
			songbook's metronome and backing pad exist to make that honest, and the
			scoring in the player tells you exactly which dimension you missed.
		</p>

		<h2>Stage 1: Count out loud</h2>
		<p>
			Start with a slow song — <a href="/song/let-it-be/">Let It Be</a> at 72 BPM is ideal —
			and play the chord changes while counting "1, 2, 3, 4" out loud. The counting does two
			things: it forces you to predict the next chord before you need it, and it makes
			slow practice feel less empty. If you cannot count and change chords at the same time,
			the chart is too fast for this stage — drop the tempo in the player until you can.
		</p>

		<h2>Stage 2: Change on the beat, not after it</h2>
		<p>
			The most common timing mistake is moving your hand after the chord lands, so the
			engine catches up a beat late. The fix is anticipation: your left hand should already
			be moving toward the next degree during the previous beat, not after the new bar
			starts.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Pick the two-chord loop in a song — <a href="/song/twinkle/">Twinkle</a>'s C–F and C–G changes are perfect.</li>
			<li>Play the loop and deliberately move your hand early, on beat 4, so the shape is locked in by beat 1.</li>
			<li>Gradually shrink that early move until it is a smooth, continuous motion.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Stage 3: Play with the backing pad</h2>
		<p>
			The metronome is unforgiving, which is exactly the point — but music is more than
			beats. Turn on the backing pad and listen to how the chords sit against the groove.
			The pad plays the song's harmony, so when you land a chord, it should fuse with the
			pad rather than clash with it. A wrong-feeling chord on beat 1 is often a right chord
			played a half-beat late.
		</p>
		<p>
			When you can play a full song in time with the pad and the metronome, try it with the
			metronome off and only the pad. Then turn everything off and keep the groove in your
			head. That is the moment the song becomes yours.
		</p>

		<div class="card cta">
			<h3>Put it into practice</h3>
			<p class="muted">
				Pick a slow song and run the three stages. Repetition builds the timing, not
				talent.
			</p>
